==Middle English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|enm|broder|brodir|brodyr|brothir|brothre|brothur|broþer|broþir|broþre|broþur}}
* {{alter|enm|broiþer|bruther||Northern}}
* {{alter|enm|broðer||early}}; {{alter|enm|broþerr||Orm}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|enm|id=male sibling|:inh|ang:brōþor}}
{{inh+|enm|ang|brōþor}}, from {{inh|enm|gmw-pro|*brōþer}}, from {{inh|enm|gem-pro|*brōþēr}}, from {{inh|enm|ine-pro|*bʰréh₂tēr}}. {{doublet|enm|frere}}. Forms with {{IPAchar|/d/}} both represent a phonological development and analogy with {{m|enm|fader|t=father}}, {{m|enm|moder|t=mother}}.

{{col-top|2|Development of the plural}}
Plural forms with {{IPAchar|/eː/}} are from late {{m+|ang|brēþre}}, with generalization of the mutated vowel from the Old English dative singular {{m|ang|brēþer}} by analogy with other nouns with mutation in both the dative singular and nominative/accusative plural such as {{m|ang|fōt}}, {{m|ang|tōþ}} ({{m|enm|fot}} and {{m|enm|toth}}), perhaps reinforced by the {{w|Old Norse}} plural {{m|non|brǿðr}}. By the end of the Early Middle English period, the final ''-e'' of this form was either lost ({{m|enm|brether}}), in conformance with both the consonantal pattern of the singular and a tendency for {{IPAchar|/(ə)lə/|/(ə)nə/|/(ə)rə/}} to develop into {{IPAchar|/əl/|/ən/|/ər/}}, or replaced with {{m|enm|-en|id=noun plural}} ({{m|enm|bretheren}}) as part of the spread of (nominative/accusative) plurals in ''-en'' at the expense of less salient ''-e''. The same processes explain the supplanting of early {{m+|enm|broþre}}, {{m|enm|broðre}} ({{m+|ang|brōþra}}, {{m|ang|brōþru}}) with ''brother'' and {{m|enm|brotheren}}, though ''brother'' may also continue  the Old English null plural {{m|ang|brōþor}}. Finally, the forms {{m|enm|brotheres}} and {{m|enm|bretheres}} plainly represent adaptation to the regular plural in {{m|enm|-es|id=noun plural}}.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|enm|/ˈbroːðər/|/ˈbroːdər/}}

===Noun===
{{enm-noun|gen=brother|gen2=brotheres|pl=bretheren|pl2=brether|pl3=brotheren}}

# A {{l|en|brother}} or [[brother-in-law]]; a [[male]] sibling.
#* {{RQ:Ormulum|d|3|url=https://digital.bodleian.ox.ac.uk/objects/90a06f70-880a-4b5b-bd30-798710afff11/surfaces/a2980d0a-ac52-4383-ba1b-bc0a6a6f567a/|Nu broþerr Ƿallꞇ͛. '''broþerͬ''' min. Affꞇ͛ þe flæsheſſ kĩde⹎ ⁊ broþerͬ mın ı crıſſtenndom. Þurrh fulluhht⹎<!--?--> ⁊ þurrh troꟕþe⹎ ⁊ broþerr mın ı ꟑodeſſ huſ {{...}}|Now, Brother Walter, my '''brother''' by blood relation and in Christendom, through baptism and faith, and my brother in God's house {{...}}}}
# A [[blood brother]]; a [[companion]] in a two-member [[loyalty]] [[pact]].
# Any [[male]] [[relative]] or [[family]] [[member]].
# A [[colleague]] or [[peer]] {{gloss|sometimes as a [[term of address]]}}:
## A [[fellow]] {{gloss|[[male]]}} [[human]] [[being]].
## A [[male]] [[friend]] or [[companion]].
## A [[male]] [[fellow]] [[Christian]] {{gloss|or other [[coreligionist]]}}.
##* {{RQ:Wycliffe NT Lichfield|Revelation|1|9|117|v|column=2|I ioon ȝoure '''bꝛoþer''' ⁊ partener in tribulacioun ⁊ kingdom ⁊ pacience in criſt iheſu .· was in an ile þat is clepid pathmos · foꝛ þe woꝛd of god · ⁊ foꝛ þe witneſſyng of iheſu|I, John, your '''brother''' and partner in tribulation, the Kingdom, and endurance in Jesus Christ, was on an island that's called Patmos for the word of God and for the witnessing of Jesus.}}
## A [[male]] [[fellow]] [[member]] of a [[guild]] or [[religious]] [[order]].
## {{lb|enm|rare}} A [[male]] [[fellow]] [[ruler]].
# [[something|Something]] [[similar]] or [[resemblant]].

====Usage notes====
* The plural form {{m|enm|brotheres}} also occurs, though less commonly. Furthermore, one occasionally encounters {{m|enm|brother}}/{{m|enm|brothere}} (especially in Early Middle English) and {{m|enm|bretheres}} (especially in Late Middle English).

