==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|aunte}}, from {{der|en|xno|aunte}}, from {{der|en|fro|ante}}, from {{der|en|la|amita||father's sister}}. Displaced native {{cog|enm|modrie||aunt}} (from {{cog|ang|mōdriġe||maternal aunt}}; compare {{cog|ang||[[faþu#Old English|faþu]], [[faþe#Old English|faþe]]|paternal aunt}}). 

{{col-top|2|Notes on pronunciation and spelling}}
The digraph {{orth|au}} representing {{IPAchar|/æ ~ ɑː/}} (rather than {{IPAchar|/ɔː/}}) is irregular. It likely reflects a development of Middle English {{IPAchar|/au̯/}} to {{IPAchar|[aː~æː]}} before {{IPAchar|/m n/}} in some varieties of Early Modern English, which later came to be identified with either {{IPAchar|/æ/}} or {{IPAchar|[æː]}} from pre-fricative lengthening (in the latter case, creating a phoneme {{IPAchar|/æː/}}<!--Dobson posits this was pronounced [aː]-->; this is the basis of the [[w:Lexical_set#Standard lexical sets for English|{{smallcaps|bath}}]] lexical set). Such pronunciations historically competed with more learned ones where {{IPAchar|/au̯/}} underwent its normal development to {{IPAchar|/ɔː/}}, with the standard language eventually adopting {{IPAchar|/æ ~ ɑː/}} in words that came to be spelt with {{orth|a}} and {{IPAchar|/ɔː/}} in those which came to be spelt with {{orth|au}} except in this word.<ref>{{R:en:Dobson:1957|II|239|793}}</ref><ref>{{R:enm:Luick:1921-1940|II|521|644-645|pageurl=644}}</ref> However, dialects may have {{IPAchar|/æ/}} or {{IPAchar|/ɑː/}} in other words such as {{m|en|launch}}, especially in the UK.

An alternative explanation derives {{IPAchar|/æ ~ ɑː/}} in this word (and other instances of {{smallcaps|bath}} before {{IPAchar|/m n/}}) from a collateral Middle English form with {{IPAchar|/a/}}, which would've undergone lengthening before nasals analogous to that occuring before fricatives. Though such a collateral form may be ancestral to pronunciations with {{IPAchar|/a ~ æ/}} in some varieties, this hypothesis cannot explain why inherited instances of {{smallcaps|bath}} before {{IPAchar|/m n/}} only derive from Middle English words which had variants in {{IPAchar|/au̯m au̯n/}}.
{{col-bottom}}

