sing.content:
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "sing"}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sengʷʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|syngen|id=to sing}}, from {{inh|en|ang|singan|t=to sing}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*singwan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*singwaną|t=to sing}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sengʷʰ-|t=to recite, sing}}.  Cognate with {{cog|stq|sjunge|t=to sing}}, {{cog|fy|sjonge|t=to sing}}, {{cog|nl|zingen|t=to sing}}, {{cog|nds-de|singen|t=to sing}}, {{cog|de|singen|t=to sing}}, {{cog|da,nb|synge|t=to sing}}, {{cog|sv|sjunga|t=to sing}}, {{cog|nn,fo,is|syngja|t=to sing}}, {{cog|grc|ὀμφή|t=voice, oracle}}.

{{listen|Česká státní hymna v G.ogg|Recorded '''singing''' from a person.}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪŋ/|[ˈsɪŋ]}}
** {{audio|en|en-uk-sing.ogg|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-sing.ogg|a=US}}
* {{a|en|pre-/ŋ/-tensing}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈsiŋ/|[ˈsiŋ]|~|[ˈsɪ̝ŋ]|a=Upper Midwestern US,Western US,CA}}
* {{a|en|non-ng-coalescence}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪŋɡ/|[ˈsɪŋɡ]|a=Northern England,Midlands}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋ|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|sing}}
* {{homophones|en|Sing|Singh}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|sang,sung|sung,sungen<l:archaic>}}<!--Sang or sung for preterite, according to AHD.-->

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[produce]] [[musical]] or [[harmonious]] [[sound]]s with one’s [[voice]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Revelation|14|3|passage=And they '''sung''' as it were a new song before the throne, and before the four beasts, and the elders, and no man could learn that song but the hundred and forty and four thousand, which were redeemed from the earth.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To perform a [[vocal]] part in a musical composition, regardless of technique.
#: {{ux|en|"I really want to '''sing''' in the school choir," said Vera.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[express]] [[audibly]] by means of a harmonious [[vocalization]].
#: {{ux|en|'''sing''' a lullaby}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852|author=Mrs M.A. Thompson|chapter=The Tutor's Daughter|title= Graham's American Monthly Magazine of Literature, Art, and Fashion| page=266| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=hdcRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA266| passage=In the lightness of my heart I '''sang''' catches of songs as my horse gayly bore me along the well-remembered road.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[soothe]] with singing.
#: {{ux|en|to '''sing''' somebody to sleep}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} Of birds, to [[vocalise]]:
## {{lb|en|ornithology}} To produce a '[[song]]', for the purposes of [[defend]]ing a [[breeding]] [[territory]] or to attract a [[mate]].
## {{lb|en|literary}} To produce any type of [[melodious]] vocalisation.
##* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXI|36|⁠I do but sing because I must,<br>And pipe but as the linnets '''sing''':<br>And unto one her note is gay,<br>⁠For now her little ones have ranged;<br>⁠And unto one her note is changed,<br>Because her brood is stol’n away.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Brækstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbjørnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=68|passage=The evening was still very warm, and the birds in the woods were '''singing''' in praise of spring.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[rejoice]].
#: {{ux|en|My heart was '''singing'''.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|idiomatic|intransitive}} To [[confess]] under [[interrogation]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make a small, shrill sound.
#: {{ux|en|The air '''sings''' in passing through a crevice.}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''singing''' kettle}}
#: {{ux|en|A strong wind was making the wires '''sing'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope Iliad|book=XXII|passage=O'er his head the flying spear / '''Sang''' innocent, and spent its force in air.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[relate]] in [[verse]]; to [[celebrate]] in [[poetry]]; to [[praise]].
#* {{RQ:Milton Poems|poem=Lycidas|page=57|passage=Who would not '''ſing''' for ''Lycidas''? he knew / Himſelf to ſing, and build the lofty rhyme.}}
#* {{RQ:Prior Solomon|page=468|passage=Again I bid the mournful Goddeſs write / The fond Purſuit of fugitive Delight: / Bid her exalt her melancholy Wing, / And rais'd from Earth, and ſav'd from Paſſion, '''ſing''' / Of human Hope by croſs Event deſtroyed, / Of uſeleſs Wealth, and Greatneſs unenjoy'd, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=26 January 1983|author=Cathy Mauk|title=Chocolate desserts to melt your Valentine’s heart|journal=[[w:The Forum of Fargo–Moorhead|The Forum]]|volume=105|issue=71|location=Fargo, N.D.|page=B-4|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/1131596869/|column=1|issn=0895-1292|oclc=9563116|passage=If chocolate is America’s most magnificent culinary obsession — and it seems to be, what with best-selling books either admitting the chocolatoholic tendencies of the author or '''singing''' the joys of the stuff — then Valentine’s Day has got to be the chocolate lover’s holiday.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|intransitive}} To display [[fine]] qualities; to stand out as [[excellent]].
#: {{ux|en|The sauce really makes this lamb '''sing'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.news.ucsb.edu/2022/020678/out-starting-gate|work=The Current|passage=[Alissa Monte said] “This result was all about demonstrating that LZ [the LUX-ZEPLIN experiment] works, and it does! As we take more data and mature our analyses, we get to make LZ '''sing'''. {{...}}”|title=‘Out of the Starting Gate’|date=2022-07-07|author=Sonia Fernandez|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707173606/https://www.news.ucsb.edu/2022/020678/out-starting-gate|archivedate=2022-07-07|accessdate=2022-07-20|publisher=University of California, Santa Barbara}}
# {{lb|en|ergative}} To be capable of being sung; to produce a certain effect by being sung.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1875|journal=Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine|volume=118|page=685
|passage=No song '''sings''' well unless it is open-vowelled, and has the rhythmic stress on the vowels. Tennyson's songs, for instance, are not generally adapted to music.}}
# {{lb|en|AU}} In traditional Aboriginal culture, to direct a [[supernatural]] influence on (a person or thing), usually malign; to [[curse]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|year_published=1947|author={{w|Ion L. Idriess}}|title={{w|Over the Range (Idriess book)|Over the Range}}|publisher={{w|Angus and Robertson}}|location=Sydney|page=212|passage=‘He says the Walcott Inlet men have '''sung''' him, and this tribe will meet the Walcott men and fight when the big walk-about comes, after the peanut season.’}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author={{w|Alex Miller (writer)|Alex Miller}}|title=Journey to the Stone Country|publisher=Allen & Unwin|year_published=2003|page=343
|passage=‘We '''sung''' them two real good. We never give Louis Beck no place to find rest from his torment.’}}
# {{lb|en|slang|archaic}} To [[yell]] or [[shout]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1851|year_published=1861|author=w:Henry Mayhew
|title=w:London Labour and the London Poor
|passage=I took my flogging like a stone. If I had '''sung''', some of the convicts would have given me some lush with a locust in it (laudanum hocussing), and when I was asleep would have given me a crack on the head that would have laid me straight.
}}

