* {{rhymes|en|ɒɹɪndʒ|ɒɹəndʒ|s=2}} {{q|see [[#Usage notes|notes]]}}
* {{hyph|en|or|ange}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[evergreen]] [[tree]] of the genus ''[[Citrus]]'' such as {{taxfmt|Citrus sinensis|species}} which yields oranges (the fruit).
# {{senseid|en|Q13191}}{{lb|en|countable}} Any round [[citrus]] [[fruit]] with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.
## {{lb|en|countable}} Specifically, a [[sweet orange]] or {{taxfmt|Citrus sinensis|species}}.
# {{senseid|en|Q39338}}{{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[colour]] of a ripe fruit of an orange tree, midway between red and yellow.
#: {{color panel|FF8000}}
#: {{syn|en|yellow-red}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} {{n-g|Various drinks:}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-03-31|author=Debbie McGowan|title=Two By Two|publisher=Beaten Track Publishing|isbn=9781910635414|page=81
|text=“What you drinking?” “'''Orange''' and soda will go down nicely, thanks.” “Pint?” “Sure.” Andy headed for the bar, stopping along the way to kiss Shaunna and check she and Kris were OK for a drink. “Everything all right?” Sean asked.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-05-07|author=Tosh Lavery|title=Tosh: An Amazing True Story Of Life, Death, Danger And Drama In The Garda Sub-Aqua Unit|publisher=Penguin UK|isbn=9781844883592
|text=I ran out into the street and around the block, searching everywhere, and finally burst into O'Dowd's pub around the corner to see Thomas sitting at the bar drinking '''orange''' and eating a bag of crisps with two old men.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2018-05-25|author=Michael Nilsen|title=Beyond the Cave|publisher=Troubador Publishing Ltd|isbn=9781789014211|page=82
|text=It transpired this lad was drinking '''orange''' and faculties were keen. There were one or two verbal exchanges, then I followed him into the car park. He said to the doorman, 'I won't be long.' He easily knocked me to the ground.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2021-06-10|author=Anna McPartlin|title=Waiting for the Miracle: Warm your heart this winter with this uplifting novel from the bestselling author of THE LAST DAYS OF RABBIT HAYES|publisher=Bonnier Zaffre Ltd.|isbn=9781838773915
|text=When the fast songs played, like the Beatles' 'Help' or The Rolling Stones' 'Satisfaction', Justin and I sat on two wooden chairs, drinking '''orange''' and holding hands. When the nuns weren't watching, I rested my head on his shoulder.}}
## An orange-coloured and orange-flavoured [[cordial]].
## An orange-coloured and orange-flavoured [[soft drink]].
## {{lb|en|uncommon}} Orange juice.
# {{lb|en|heraldry}} A rarely-used nontraditional tincture found in Catalan, South African, and French municipal heraldry.
# {{lb|en|heraldry}} An orange-coloured [[roundel]].
#* {{see citations|en}}
# An orange dye or pigment. 
# A pen, pencil, crayon, etc., made of orange pigment.
# Something, or a part of a thing, which is orange.

====Usage notes====
* It is commonly stated that ''orange'' has no rhymes. While there are no commonly used English dictionary words that rhyme exactly with ''orange'' ({{m|en|door hinge}} comes close in US pronunciation), see [[Rhymes:English/ɒɹɪndʒ]] for some possibilities. See also [[w:Orange_(word)#Rhyme|the Wikipedia article about rhymes for the word ''orange'']].
* In most dialects, ''orange'' is pronounced with two syllables.  But in certain dialects of North American English, the vowel of the second syllable is deleted and the word is pronounced as one syllable.<ref>https://www.merriam-webster.com/words-at-play/what-rhymes-with-orange</ref> In such dialects, the two forms are generally {{l|en|allophony|allophonic}}.

