favourite.content:
==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|favorite||American English}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:bor|frm:favorit<ety:inh<fro:favorit<ety:bor<it:favorito>>>:bor<it:favorito>>|tree=1}}
Attested from the 1580s, borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|favorit}}, from {{der|en|fro|favorit}} or {{der|en|it|favorito|t=favoured, favourite}}, past participle of {{der|en|it|favorire|t=to favor}}, from {{m|it|favore}}, from {{der|en|la|favor||good will, favor}}, from the verb {{m|la|faveō||to favour}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-|*bʰeh₂weh₁yeti|to be favourable to}}, from the root {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-||to shine, glow light}}. {{doublet|en|favori}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfeɪv.ɹɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-favourite.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 favourite.ogg|a=AU}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|more|sup=most,favouritemost,est<l:nonstandard>}} {{tlb|en|British|Ireland|Commonwealth}}

# {{lb|en|[[superlative]]}} Most-[[liked]]. (''In other words,'' [person]'s favorite [noun] = the [noun] that [person] likes the most.)
#: {{ant|en|[[least]] [[favorite]]|unfavourite|unfavorite}}
#: {{ux|en|This is my '''favourite''' day!}}
#: {{ux|en|I saw a hilariously funny movie with all of my '''favourite''' comedians in it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Brigid Lowry|title=Guitar Highway Rose|passage=''The Nanny'' is one of Pippa's '''favourite''' shows, but tonight the pleasure of mindlessly blobbing out in her usual Sunday-laid-back-luxury style...}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=w:Hadley Freeman|title=Paul Rudd on Ant-Man, being Hollywood’s go-to nice guy and growing up with English parents in Kansas|url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/2015/jul/09/paul-rudd-ant-man-hollywoods-nice-guy-english-parents-kansas|date=2015-07-09|passage=Then there are the characters Rudd describes as “the full-on buffoons”, such as reporter Brian Fantana in Anchorman, who gives names to his testicles and – still my '''favourite''' Rudd performance – Andy the obnoxious summer camp counsellor in cult 2001 comedy Wet Hot American Summer, which is being revived on Netflix this summer, in which Rudd will co-star alongside original cast members including Poehler and Bradley Cooper.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180301050956/https://www.theguardian.com/film/2015/jul/09/paul-rudd-ant-man-hollywoods-nice-guy-english-parents-kansas}}
# {{lb|en|[[positive]]|high-register or archaic}} Liked, cherished.
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|volume=V|letter=VII|chapter=Mr. Lovelace. In Continuation|page=87|passage=What joy has this dog in miſchief!—More than I can have in the completion of my moſt '''favourite''' purpoſes!}}

====Usage notes====
* In mainstream current usage, ''favourite'' functions as a [[superlative]] adjective,<ref>Sailer, Manfred, and Nicolas Lamoure. Superlative ever in Dutch, French, German, and Spanish. Universitätsbibliothek Johann Christian Senckenberg, 2023.</ref> even though it is not ''morphologically'' superlative—in so far as it lacks a corresponding positive form meaning 'liked' nor comparative form meaning 'more liked'. This is reflected in the direct antonym being ''[[least]] favourite'' (which is transparently superlative), as well as in the fact that the phrase "more favourite" is rare outside second-language or high-register usage, and would indeed sound [[stilted]] to some native speakers.

