# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[make#Verb|make]] (something) [[appear]] like an [[apple#Noun|apple]] ''({{senseno|en|Q89}})''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=w:Marilyn Strathern|chapter=Enterprising Kinship: Consumer Choice and the New Reproductive Technologies|title=Reproducing the Future: Essays on Anthropology, Kinship and the New Reproductive Technologies|location=Manchester|publisher=w:Manchester University Press|section=part I|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BM9RAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA38|isbn=978-0-7190-3673-6|passage=To choose responsibly, our active citizen must know what is being offered, much of this knowledge being filtered through appearance: things must look what they are supposed to be. Apples must look like applies. One might say they have to be '''appled'''-up; varieties are selected for marketing which have the most apple-like qualities.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Claudia D. Newcorn|chapter=4|title=Crossover|series=Chronicles of Feyree|seriesvolume=scroll 1|location=[United States]|publisher=Theogony Books|year_published=2021|page=35|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cdMnEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT62|isbn=978-1-942936-65-7|passage=A large smile '''appled''' his full cheeks as the four sprytes eagerly served themselves from the seeds and thinly sliced fruits.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To [[become]] like an apple.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=w:Gregory David Roberts|chapter=1|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5JaxSJlpGmAC&pg=PT37|title=[[w:Shantaram (novel)|Shantaram]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hachette Book Group|Hachette Digital]]|year_published=2012|section=part 1|isbn=978-1-405-51697-6|passage=He glanced at me, his cheeks '''appled''' in the impish grin I was learning to recognise as the clever under-side of his broad and gentle smile.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Cynthia Robinson|chapter=10|title=The Barbary Dogs|series=A [[w:Thomas Dunne Books|Thomas Dunne Book]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Minotaur Books, [[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Publishing Group]]|page=57|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gGn4TxwXmFUC&pg=PA57|isbn=978-0-312-55974-8|passage=She smiled, and her cheeks '''appled''' up and her teeth were big and flat and her mouth was wide and spacious like an open invitation.}}
