===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person, typically someone other than a [[family]] [[member]], [[spouse]] or [[lover]], whose company one enjoys and towards whom one feels [[affection]].
#* {{RQ:Austen Emma|passage="[…]if you have any wish to speak openly to me as a '''friend''', or to ask my opinion of any thing that you may have in contemplation—as a '''friend''', indeed, you may command me.—I will hear whatever you like. I will tell you exactly what I think." "As a '''friend'''!"—repeated Mr. Knightley.—"Emma, that I fear is a word—No, I have no wish—[…]”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author=w:Ralph Waldo Emerson|chapter=Friendship|title={{w|Essays: First Series|Essays}}|volume=I
|passage=The only reward of virtue is virtue; the only way to have a '''friend''' is to be one.}}
#* '''1917''', Richard M. Gummere translating {{w|Seneca the Younger|Seneca}} as ''{{w|Epistulae Morales ad Lucilium|Ad Lucilium Epistulae Morales}}'', Loeb Classical Library, Vol. I, [[:s:Moral letters to Lucilius/Letter 3|No. 3]]:
#*: ...if you consider any man a '''friend''' whom you do not trust as you trust yourself, you are mightily mistaken and you do not sufficiently understand what true [[friendship]] means.
#* '''1923''', William Armistead Falconer translating {{w|Cicero}} as ''[https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cicero/Laelius_de_Amicitia/text*.html De Amicitia]'', Loeb Classical Library, Vol. XX, p. 34:
#*: ...he who looks upon a true '''friend''', looks, as it were, upon a sort of image of himself. Wherefore '''friends''', though absent, are at hand; though in need, yet abound; though weak, are strong; and&mdash;harder saying still&mdash;though dead, are yet alive; so great is the esteem on the part of their '''friends''', the tender recollection and the deep longing that still attends them.
#* '''1927 Mar. 31''', {{w|Ernest Hemingway}}, [https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=SgKeQfmimJEC&pg=PA248 letter] to {{w|F. Scott Fitzgerald}}:
#*: ...you are my devoted '''friend''' too. You do more and work harder and oh shit I'd get [[maudlin]] about how damned [[swell]] you are. My god I'd like to see you... You're a hell of a good guy.
#* '''1933 Dec. 12''', {{w|Walter Winchell}}, "On Broadway", ''Scranton Republican'', p. 5:
#*: Definition of a '''friend''': One who walks in&mdash;when the rest of the world walks out.
#: {{ux|en|John and I have been '''friends''' ever since we were roommates at college.}}
#: {{ux|en|Trust is important between '''friends'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I used to find it hard to make '''friends''' when I was shy.}}
#: {{ux|en|We became '''friends''' in the war and remain '''friends''' to this day.}}
#: {{ux|en|We were '''friends''' with some girls from the other school and stayed '''friends''' with them.}}
# An [[associate]] who provides [[assistance]]; patron, mentor.
#* {{quote-book|en|format=letter|year=1779|author={{w|Benjamin Franklin}}|title=[[s:The Whistle (Franklin, 1779)|The Whistle]]|text=When I was a child of seven years old, my '''friends''', on a holiday, filled my little pocket with halfpence.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|text= The vicious propensities—the want of principle which he was careful to guard from the knowledge of his best '''friend''', could not escape the observation of a young man of nearly the same age with himself, and who had opportunities of seeing him in unguarded moments, which Mr. Darcy could not have.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|format=poem|year=1907|author={{w|Hilaire Belloc}}|title=[[s:Cautionary Tales for Children/Jim|Jim]]|text= There was a Boy whose name was Jim; His '''Friends''' were very good to him. They gave him Tea, and Cakes, and Jam, And slices of delicious Ham, And Chocolate with pink inside, And little Tricycles to ride, And read him Stories through and through, And even took him to the Zoo—}}
#: {{ux|en|The Automobile Association is every motorist's '''friend'''.&nbsp;&nbsp; The police is every law-abiding citizen's '''friend'''.}}
# A person with whom one is [[vaguely]] or [[indirectly]] [[acquainted]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-21|author=w:Oliver Burkeman
|volume=189|issue=2|page=27|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=The tao of tech
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google
|passage=The dirty secret of the internet is that all this distraction and interruption is immensely profitable. Web companies like to boast about [&hellip;], or offering services that let you "stay up to date with what your '''friends''' are doing",{{...}}and so on. But the real way to build a successful online business is to be better than your rivals at undermining people's control of their own attention.}}
#: {{coi|en|a '''friend''' of a '''friend'''}}
#: {{ux|en|I added him as a '''friend''' on Facebook, but I hardly know him.}}
# A person who backs or supports something.
#: {{ux|en|I’m not a '''friend''' of cheap wine.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} An object or idea that can be used for good.
#: {{ux|en|Fruit is your '''friend'''.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|ironic|used only in the [[vocative]]}} {{n-g|Used as a form of [[address]] when [[warn]]ing someone.}}
#: {{ux|en|You’d better watch it, '''friend'''.}}
# {{lb|en|object-oriented programming}} A [[function]] or [[class]] granted special access to the [[private]] and [[protected]] [[member]]s of another class.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=Tom Swan|title=Learning C++|passage=But don't take the following sections as an endorsement of '''friends'''. Top C++ programmers avoid using '''friends''' unless absolutely necessary.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Stephen Prata|title=C++ primer plus|passage=In that case, the function needn't (and shouldn't) be a '''friend'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=D S Malik|title=C++ Programming: From Problem Analysis to Program Design|passage=To make a function be a '''friend''' to a class, the reserved word <code>friend</code> precedes the function prototype{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|climbing}} A [[spring-loaded camming device]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|title=Rock Climbing Basics
|passage=Since they were introduced in the 1970s, '''friends''' have revolutionized climbing, making protection possible in previously impossible places {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|euphemistic}} A [[lover]]; a [[boyfriend]] or [[girlfriend]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|5|2|passage=Nor to the motion of a schoolboy's tongue ; Nor never come in visard to my '''friend'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1813|author=Samuel Foote|title=The Commissary, Etc.|page=17
|passage=Time has been, when a gentleman wanted a '''friend''', I could supply him with choice in an hour; but the market is spoiled, and a body might as soon produce a hare or a partridge {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1975|author=w:Janis Ian|title=In the Winter
|passage=I met your '''friend'''. She's very nice, what can I say?}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|obsolete}} A [[relative]], a [[relation]] by blood or marriage.
#: '''''Friends''' agree best at a distance.''<!--James Kelly, ''A Complete Collection of Scottish Proverbs'' (1721): "By friends here is meant relations."-->
#: ''Make '''friends''' of framet folk.''<!--James Kelly, ''A Complete Collection of Scottish Proverbs'' (1721): "Spoken to dissuade people from marrying their near kinswomen."-->
#* '''1895''', Crockett, ''Bog-Myrtle'', 232:
#*: He was not a drop's blood to me, though him and my wife were far-out '''friends'''.
# {{lb|en|in the plural|usually preceded by "and"}} {{ng|Used to refer collectively to a group of associated individuals, especially those comprising a cast, company, or crew}}
#: {{syn|en|company|alt1=and company|et al.}}
#: {{ux|en|Meanwhile on ''The View'', Whoopi Goldberg and '''friends''' continue to spout their takes on the hot topics of the day.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020|author=Beth Van Schaack|title=Imagining Justice for Syria|isbn=9780190055967|page=98
|text=Russia and '''friends''' continued to criticize the work of the JIM, focusing in particular on the methodology employed and the decision of the experts not to go to certain on-site visits for security reasons{{nb...}}}}

====Usage notes====
* ''friends'' is found as an invariable plural in the phrases ''make friends with'' and ''be friends with'': ''I am '''friends''' with her''; ''He made '''friends''' with his co-worker.''
* We usually ''[[make]] a friend'', or ''make friends with'' someone. See '''[[:Appendix:Collocations of do, have, make, and take]]'''
* In older texts and certain dialects, the prepositional phrase '''to friend''' means "as a friend or an ally", for exampleː "[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Faerie_Queene/Book_I/Canto_I with God to frend] (Spenser)". The antonym to the phrase '''to friend''' is '''to [[fiend]]'''.

